Unless the person needing wrecker services specifically requests that a wrecker service unauthorized under this article be called, a wrecker service shall not be called upon, by police officers or other authorized employees of the county, to furnish the service of towing any vehicle upon the streets of the city or county, unless the wrecker service shall have complied with the following conditions:
(1) The wrecker service shall be available twenty-four (24) hours a day, seven (7) days a week, to provide the service;
(2) The wrecker service shall have qualified licensed employees, skilled and knowledgeable in performing their duties, and available at all times to avoid delay;
(3) The wrecker service shall operate at least two (2) wreckers, at least one (1) of which shall be an eight thousand (8,000) pound dual cable wrecker and at least one (1) of which shall be a "rollback" wrecker. The wreckers shall be in good operating condition, meeting all requirements of state law, and being equipped with a flashing light mounted on top of the tow car, or on top of the crane or hoist, which light will be visible for a distance of one thousand (1,000) feet, and mounted in such a manner that it can be seen, both by approaching and rear traffic, and also, sufficient red flares, lanterns or warning lights and reflectors, to be able to mark the scene of a wreck on both sides of the same, so as to warn all traffic on the highway of the emergency existing while the wreckers are in operation. The wreckers shall also carry brooms, shovels, and fire extinguishers suitable for extinguishing fires in wrecked vehicles and for cleaning up the debris and glass from the area where the wreck has occurred. The fire extinguisher shall be of a type capable of extinguishing a fire of flammable liquid. Wrecker services on large wrecker calls shall have available heavy duty wrecker/s having a twenty-five thousand (25,000) pounds minimum gross weight rating with a twenty-ton winch or an equivalent wrecker having same minimum capabilities capable of moving vehicles, including large trucks, buses and loaded and unloaded tractor trailers;
(4) The wrecker service shall have attached and displayed in a conspicuous place, on both right and left sides of a tow car, the name of the towing service/company. No magnetic or removable decals or signs shall be allowed.
(5) The wrecker service shall maintain in the wrecker service's name at a minimum the following insurance policies: (i) an automobile liability insurance policy having a combined single limit of not less than three hundred thousand dollars ($300,000.00), (ii) a comprehensive general liability policy providing bodily injury coverage limits of one hundred thousand dollars ($100,000.00) per person and one hundred thousand dollars ($100,000.00) per occurrence and property damage coverage limits of one hundred thousand dollars ($100,000.00) per person and one hundred thousand dollars ($100,000.00) annual aggregate, (iii) cargo and garage keepers liability insurance with a limit of one hundred thousand dollars ($100,000.00), and (iv) workers' compensation insurance in the minimum coverage required by Georgia law. Said insurance shall name Floyd County as an additional insured under the policy and shall provide for written notification within fifteen (15) days of policy modification, expiration or cancellation. The wrecker service shall provide to the chief of police a full copy of the insurance policy no later than five (5) days from date of execution of agreement or extension thereof or any modification of the policy and upon renewal of the policy;
(6) The wrecker service shall provide a storage area for the storage and safekeeping of vehicles towed under this article. The area shall be enclosed with a solid wall, or a substantial wire fence. Such fence shall not be less than eight (8) feet in height, except that all wrecker services in operation as of November 15, 1993, may maintain a fence not less than six (6) feet in height until such time as the service elects to construct a new or replacement fence. All fences must be visually screened, and have a capacity for a minimum of thirty (30) cars;
(7) The wrecker service's operating personnel of wrecker units shall be technically qualified and physically capable of responding immediately to all calls received;
(8) The wrecker service must maintain registration with the Georgia Public Service Commission and possess all state-required permits;
(9) The wrecker service must obtain through the Floyd County Police Department identification cards for each operator which shall be worn at all times while responding to calls under this article. The wrecker operator must provide the Floyd County Police Department a copy of the operator's driver's license prior to issuance of an identification card. Such identification card must be renewed within three (3) years of its issuance.
(10) Wreckers must pass an annual inspection to be performed by the Motor Carrier Compliance Division of the Georgia Department of Public Safety. A copy of this inspection and the results thereof will be submitted annually to the Floyd County Police Department by December 31 st of each given year.
(Ord. No. 2010-001A, 6-8-10; Res. of 8-23-22, §§ 1—3; Ord. No. 2-18-2022, §§ 1—3, 8-23-22)

824:
(a) In order for a wrecker service to maintain eligibility for Floyd County's rotating schedule of calls to wrecker services, the wrecker service shall:
(1) Maintain a daily list of vehicles towed and/or stored for police-initiated towing and storage services. The list shall contain details as to the make, year, model, color, vehicle I.D. number, license plate number, name of owner, and the purpose of removing and/or storing vehicle. The records shall be retained for a period of twelve (12) months and shall be made available to the police department at any reasonable hour;
(2) Be responsible to the vehicle owner for all damages done during towing and storage of any vehicles entrusted to them under this article;
(3) Have a response time of thirty (30) minutes or less for at least ninety (90) percent of all calls during every calendar year. Response time is the time elapsed from the time the 911 center dispatches the wrecker service until the time that the wrecker actually arrives at the location of the vehicle which is to be towed;
(4) tow vehicles in a professional manner in accordance with standards in the contract and deal with the public in a professional and courteous manner;
(5) take reasonable care to ensure no further damage will occur to the vehicle towed either through carelessness or from natural elements, and the wrecker service shall use the proper equipment of whatever nature necessary (i.e. dollies, wheel lifts etc.) in order to prevent further damage to the vehicles towed;
(6) Shall cooperate with all duly authorized law enforcement officers, in the protection of any evidence or in any investigation arising out of a wreck or seizure of a vehicle by any officials;
(7) Pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 40-6-276, prior to leaving the scene of any collision, the responding wrecker service shall clean the pavement and right-of-way of all debris of the collision, including all glass off paved roadway; In the event that engine fluids have been spilled on the roadway the wrecker service shall provide and spread only that amount necessary of oil dry material on the affected area in order to render the roadway safe for vehicular traffic.
(8) release any vehicle to the registered owner upon payment unless a hold has been placed on the vehicle by the arresting officer or a court order prohibits the release;
(9) Not go to any accident scene unless:
a. Called by the police department;
b. Called by the vehicle owner/operator; or
c. There exists an extreme emergency immediately endangering life or property;
(10) Whenever any wrecked or abandoned vehicle is removed without the request, direction or participation of the police department, if the owner is unknown, within seventy-two (72) hours of such removal, furnish to the police department on a form supplied or approved by the police department the following information with respect to such vehicle: tag number and description, including year, model, make, color and vehicle identification number and upon receipt of such information, the police department shall immediately take steps to ascertain the identity of the vehicle owner and notify him of the time and place of removal and of the present whereabouts of the vehicle.
(b) Nothing in this article shall relieve any wrecker service of any of the requirements imposed by virtue of O.C.G.A. § 40-11-1 et seq. or by virtue of any other law with respect to the duties, among others, to make diligent inquiries as to the ownership of vehicles and notification to owners.
(c) In order for a wrecker service to maintain eligibility for Floyd County's rotating schedule of calls to wrecker services, on call wrecker services shall be subject to monthly inspections of insurance and required equipment to insure compliance with this article.
(Ord. No. 2010-001A, 6-8-10)

